/* Bassiet book, extra strata for Depths 1, 5 and 6: The Furnace Ledgers (c1-metab), The Apothecary of Knives (c5-drugs), The Sleeping Draughts (c6-sleepers). Original text. Loads after depths.js and the other books; pushes its strata into DEPTHS once (guarded against a double push). */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
(function(){
var NEW=[
 {n:1,s:{id:'metab',name:'The Furnace Ledgers',ch:'c1-metab',topic:'Metabolism'}},
 {n:5,s:{id:'drugs',name:'The Apothecary of Knives',ch:'c5-drugs',topic:'Antimicrobial pharmacology'}},
 {n:6,s:{id:'sleepers',name:'The Sleeping Draughts',ch:'c6-sleepers',topic:'Anaesthesia and analgesia'}}];
function apply(D){
  if(!D||!D.length)return;
  NEW.forEach(function(x){
    var d=D[x.n-1];if(!d||!d.strata)return;
    var have=d.strata.some(function(s){return s.id===x.s.id||(s.ch||('c'+d.n+'-'+s.id))===x.s.ch});
    if(!have)d.strata.push({id:x.s.id,name:x.s.name,ch:x.s.ch,topic:x.s.topic});
  });
}
/* bookx1.js loads after depths.js and every book file, so DEPTHS already exists here. No hooks. */
if(Array.isArray(window.DEPTHS))apply(window.DEPTHS);
})();

BOOK['c1-metab']={depth:1,title:'The Furnace Ledgers',
epi:'Every furnace keeps a ledger. Read what piled up and what ran short, and you can name the door that jammed.',
c:['g6pd','fatty-acids','ammonia','nucleotides','glycogen-storage','lysosomal','hypoglycaemia'],
S:[
{h:'The side door: the pentose phosphate pathway',p:[
'Glucose-6-phosphate can turn aside from glycolysis into the **pentose phosphate pathway** (the hexose monophosphate shunt), which runs in the cytoplasm and makes no ATP. Its **oxidative phase** is irreversible: **glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)**, the rate-limiting enzyme, starts a sequence that yields **two NADPH** and one CO₂. Its **non-oxidative phase** is a reversible reshuffling of sugars by **transketolase** (which needs thiamine): it makes ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotides or hands carbon back to glycolysis.',
'NADPH is reducing power, not energy. Red cells have no other source of it, and they need it to keep **glutathione** reduced, because reduced glutathione is what neutralises hydrogen peroxide before it oxidises haemoglobin. **G6PD deficiency** is X-linked recessive and the commonest human enzyme defect; it is frequent where falciparum malaria was common, because it gives partial protection. An oxidant stress tips the cells over: infection (the commonest trigger), fava beans or a drug. Haemoglobin precipitates as **Heinz bodies**, the spleen bites them out (**bite cells**), and one to three days after exposure there is an intravascular haemolysis with dark urine and jaundice.'],
table:{cols:['Where NADPH is spent','What it does','When it fails'],rows:[
['Glutathione reductase','keeps glutathione reduced to neutralise H₂O₂','oxidant haemolysis (G6PD deficiency)'],
['Fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis','electrons for fatty acid synthase and HMG-CoA reductase','—'],
['Steroid synthesis (adrenal, gonads)','cytochrome P450 hydroxylations','—'],
['NADPH oxidase (phagocytes)','superoxide for the respiratory burst','chronic granulomatous disease (the oxidase itself is missing)'],
['Nitric oxide synthase','NO from arginine','—']]},
key:'G6PD → NADPH → reduced glutathione → safe haemoglobin. Break the first link and an infection or an oxidant drug breaks the red cell.',
s:[['path','Heinz bodies need a supravital stain (crystal violet); bite cells and blister cells show on the ordinary smear. Measure the enzyme two to three months after a crisis: reticulocytes carry more G6PD, so a test during haemolysis can look falsely normal.'],
['pharm','Classic oxidant triggers: primaquine and tafenoquine (test G6PD before giving them for P. vivax), dapsone, sulfonamides, nitrofurantoin and rasburicase. Methylene blue needs NADPH to work, so in G6PD deficiency it fails to treat methaemoglobinaemia and can itself cause haemolysis.'],
['bio','Erythrocyte transketolase activity is a functional test of thiamine status: it is low in deficiency and rises when thiamine is added to the sample.'],
['micro','In chronic granulomatous disease the danger comes from catalase-positive organisms (Staphylococcus aureus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia cepacia, Serratia, Nocardia), which destroy the hydrogen peroxide that the crippled phagocyte might otherwise borrow from them.']]},

{h:'Building fat and burning it',p:[
'Fat is made in the cytoplasm and burned in the mitochondria. To **make** fat after a meal, the liver exports acetyl-CoA from the mitochondrion as **citrate**. In the cytoplasm **acetyl-CoA carboxylase**, the rate-limiting enzyme, uses **biotin** to make **malonyl-CoA**; insulin and citrate switch it on, glucagon and AMP-activated kinase switch it off. **Fatty acid synthase** then builds palmitate two carbons at a time, spending NADPH.',
'To **burn** fat in a fast, long-chain fatty acids must be carried into the mitochondrion by the **carnitine shuttle**; its first enzyme, **carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT I)**, is rate-limiting and is **inhibited by malonyl-CoA**, so a cell cannot make and burn fat at once. Each turn of **β-oxidation** cuts off two carbons as acetyl-CoA and yields one FADH₂ and one NADH. Medium chains enter without carnitine; very long and branched chains are first shortened in **peroxisomes** (Zellweger, adrenoleukodystrophy and Refsum disease: see the Nucleus Vault); odd chains end as propionyl-CoA, which becomes succinyl-CoA through a vitamin B12 enzyme.',
'When β-oxidation fails, a fasting child can make neither ketones nor enough glucose (gluconeogenesis runs on the ATP that fat supplies). **MCAD deficiency** (autosomal recessive, the commonest of these defects) presents after a fast or a vomiting illness with **hypoketotic hypoglycaemia**, lethargy, seizures and sometimes sudden death. The rule for all of them: never let the child fast.'],
table:{cols:['Defect','Clue','Avoid or give'],rows:[
['MCAD','hypoketotic hypoglycaemia after fasting; raised C8 acylcarnitine; dicarboxylic aciduria','avoid fasting; IV glucose early in illness'],
['Primary carnitine deficiency (OCTN2 transporter)','very low plasma carnitine; hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy, weakness','oral L-carnitine'],
['CPT II (adult form)','myalgia, myoglobinuria after prolonged exercise, fasting or cold','carbohydrate before exercise'],
['Long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD)','hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, cardiomyopathy, retinopathy; a carrier mother carrying an affected fetus risks acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP','avoid fasting; low long-chain fat diet with medium-chain triglycerides'],
['Peroxisomal defects','very-long-chain or branched fatty acids cannot be shortened','see the Nucleus Vault, Shipping and sorting']]},
key:'Fasting hypoglycaemia with inappropriately low ketones means fat is not being burned (MCAD, carnitine) or insulin is still present. If ketones are appropriately high, look instead at glycogen and the counter-regulatory hormones.',
s:[['bio','Biotin is the cofactor of the carboxylases: acetyl-CoA, pyruvate and propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin, so very large intakes of raw egg can cause deficiency: dermatitis, alopecia and organic acids in the urine.'],
['pharm','Valproate depletes carnitine and inhibits β-oxidation; L-carnitine is given for valproate-induced hyperammonaemia and liver injury. Hypoglycin A in unripe ackee fruit blocks acyl-CoA dehydrogenases and causes severe hypoglycaemia (Jamaican vomiting sickness).'],
['phys','The resting heart takes most of its energy from fatty acids, which is why carnitine and long-chain oxidation defects can present as cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia.']]},

{h:'Ketones: the fuel of hunger',p:[
'In a long fast the liver burns fat at full speed, but its oxaloacetate is drained into gluconeogenesis, so acetyl-CoA cannot enter the TCA cycle and is turned into **ketone bodies** instead. Mitochondrial **HMG-CoA synthase** (rate-limiting; the cytoplasmic isoform starts cholesterol) makes HMG-CoA, which is split to **acetoacetate**. Acetoacetate is reduced to **β-hydroxybutyrate** or loses CO₂ to give **acetone**, the fruity breath.',
'The liver makes ketones but cannot burn them: it lacks the transferase (thiophorase) that reactivates acetoacetate. Muscle, heart and kidney use them; after a few days of starvation so does the **brain**, sparing muscle protein. **Red cells** have no mitochondria and always need glucose. Even-chain fat cannot become net glucose, because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible; only glycerol and odd-chain propionyl-CoA feed gluconeogenesis (the fasting timetable is in the Furnaces).'],
table:{cols:['State','Ketones','Glucose','Clue'],rows:[
['Starvation ketosis','moderately ↑','low to normal','some insulin remains, so the acidosis is mild; carbohydrate corrects it'],
['Diabetic ketoacidosis','↑↑, mostly β-hydroxybutyrate','high','no insulin; high anion gap; serum K⁺ often high although body K⁺ is depleted'],
['Euglycaemic ketoacidosis','↑↑','normal or only mildly raised','SGLT2 inhibitors, pregnancy, insulin-treated patients who stop eating'],
['Alcoholic ketoacidosis','↑↑, nearly all β-hydroxybutyrate','low or normal','binge, then vomiting and no food; urine dipstick weak; thiamine, glucose and fluids'],
['Fat-oxidation defect or hyperinsulinism','low or absent','low','hypoketotic hypoglycaemia (see above)']]},
key:'The liver makes ketones but cannot burn them; red cells cannot burn them at all. The brain learns to, given a few days.',
s:[['phys','Ketoacids give a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with deep, fast Kussmaul breathing. In diabetic ketoacidosis there is no insulin to restrain lipolysis; in starvation some insulin remains, so the acidosis stays mild.'],
['bio','The urine nitroprusside test detects acetoacetate but not β-hydroxybutyrate. In alcoholic ketoacidosis, excess NADH pushes most ketones to β-hydroxybutyrate, so the dipstick can be weak while the patient is very acidotic; measure blood β-hydroxybutyrate.'],
['pharm','SGLT2 inhibitors cause euglycaemic ketoacidosis because glucose leaves in the urine, insulin doses are cut and glucagon rises, so ketones climb while the glucose looks reassuring; stop them before surgery and in acute illness. In any ketoacidosis treated with insulin, replace potassium first if it is below about 3.5 mmol/L, because insulin drives K⁺ into cells.']]},

{h:'The urea cycle: paying for nitrogen',p:[
'Amino acid nitrogen cannot be stored. **Transaminases** (ALT and AST, both using **vitamin B6**) pass amino groups to α-ketoglutarate to make glutamate, and in liver mitochondria **glutamate dehydrogenase** releases them as ammonia. Muscle sends its nitrogen to the liver as **alanine**, other tissues as **glutamine**.',
'The liver turns ammonia into **urea**. In the mitochondrion, **carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS I)**, the rate-limiting enzyme, joins ammonia and bicarbonate, but only when its activator **N-acetylglutamate** is present. **Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC)** adds carbamoyl phosphate to ornithine to make citrulline. In the cytoplasm aspartate brings the second nitrogen, the product splits into arginine and fumarate, and **arginase** cuts arginine into urea and ornithine, which returns to start again.',
'When the cycle fails, ammonia drives glutamate to glutamine in astrocytes, which swell. A newborn becomes sleepy, vomits and breathes fast (a **respiratory alkalosis** is an early clue) a day or two after protein feeds begin, then fits and slips into coma. **OTC deficiency** is the commonest urea cycle defect and the only X-linked one. Its trapped carbamoyl phosphate spills into pyrimidine synthesis and becomes **orotic acid**, which is why urinary orotic acid is high in OTC deficiency and not in CPS I deficiency.'],
table:{cols:['Block','Inheritance','Ammonia','Distinguishing clue'],rows:[
['N-acetylglutamate synthase','AR','↑↑','like CPS I deficiency, but responds to carglumic acid'],
['CPS I','AR','↑↑','orotic acid normal or low; citrulline low'],
['OTC','X-linked','↑↑','orotic acid ↑, citrulline low; newborn boys, carrier girls with protein aversion and later episodes'],
['Argininosuccinate synthetase (citrullinaemia)','AR','↑↑','citrulline very high'],
['Argininosuccinate lyase','AR','↑','argininosuccinic acid ↑; brittle, nodular hair (trichorrhexis nodosa)'],
['Arginase','AR','mild ↑','progressive spastic diplegia; arginine ↑'],
['UMP synthase (orotic aciduria; a pyrimidine defect)','AR','normal','orotic acid ↑; megaloblastic anaemia that B12 and folate do not correct; give uridine']]},
key:'Ammonia high + orotic acid high = OTC deficiency. Orotic acid high + ammonia normal + megaloblastic anaemia = UMP synthase deficiency, a pyrimidine defect.',
s:[['pharm','Lowering ammonia: restrict protein and give calories to stop catabolism; **lactulose** (colonic bacteria acidify it and trap NH₄⁺ in the gut); **rifaximin** (kills ammonia-making flora); **sodium benzoate and phenylbutyrate** (carry nitrogen out as hippurate and phenylacetylglutamine); arginine in most urea cycle defects; haemodialysis in a crisis. Valproate can raise ammonia with normal liver tests.'],
['path','Hepatic encephalopathy in cirrhosis is the same chemistry in an adult: tremor, asterixis, slurred speech and confusion. Common triggers are gastrointestinal bleeding (a protein load), infection, constipation, hypokalaemia, dehydration and sedatives.'],
['phys','Hypokalaemia worsens hyperammonaemia: K⁺ leaves tubular cells in exchange for H⁺, the more acidic cells make more ammonia, and the excess enters the renal vein.'],
['bio','The two cycles are linked: fumarate released in the cytoplasm becomes malate and oxaloacetate, and oxaloacetate is transaminated back to aspartate to bring in the next nitrogen.']]},

{h:'Building the letters: purines and pyrimidines',p:[
'Nucleotides are built new (de novo) or salvaged from recycled bases; both routes use **PRPP**, made from pentose-shunt ribose-5-phosphate. **Purines** (adenine, guanine) are built **on the sugar**: the ring is assembled atom by atom on PRPP, starting with the rate-limiting **glutamine-PRPP amidotransferase**, from glycine, glutamine, aspartate, CO₂ and folate one-carbon units. The first finished purine, **IMP**, branches to AMP and, through **IMP dehydrogenase**, to GMP. **Pyrimidines** are built the other way round, ring first: cytoplasmic **CPS II** starts it, the ring closes into **orotic acid**, and **UMP synthase** then adds the sugar. **Ribonucleotide reductase** makes every deoxyribonucleotide, and **thymidylate synthase** makes dTMP with a folate that **dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)** must regenerate.',
'Salvage is cheaper: **HGPRT** puts hypoxanthine and guanine back onto PRPP; spare purines become uric acid through **xanthine oxidase**. In **Lesch-Nyhan syndrome** (X-linked, absent HGPRT) uric acid soars: orange crystals in the nappy, gout and stones, with dystonia, intellectual disability and compulsive biting of lips and fingers. In **adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency** dATP accumulates in lymphocytes, shuts down ribonucleotide reductase and causes severe combined immunodeficiency.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Notes'],rows:[
['Methotrexate','human DHFR','cancer, rheumatoid arthritis, ectopic pregnancy; mucositis, marrow suppression, liver fibrosis, pneumonitis; leucovorin rescues normal cells'],
['Trimethoprim, pyrimethamine','bacterial or protozoal DHFR','selective for the microbe\'s enzyme; megaloblastic changes at high dose'],
['5-fluorouracil','thymidylate synthase (as FdUMP)','colorectal and other solid tumours; leucovorin enhances it; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity'],
['Hydroxyurea','ribonucleotide reductase','sickle cell disease (raises HbF), myeloproliferative neoplasms'],
['Mycophenolate','IMP dehydrogenase','lymphocytes depend on de novo GMP; transplant, lupus nephritis; teratogen'],
['Leflunomide','dihydroorotate dehydrogenase','rheumatoid arthritis; teratogen, hepatotoxic'],
['Azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine','purine analogues','TPMT activity guides the dose; xanthine oxidase inactivates them, so cut the dose sharply with allopurinol'],
['Allopurinol, febuxostat','xanthine oxidase','gout, tumour lysis prevention; HLA-B*58:01 raises the risk of SJS/TEN with allopurinol']]},
key:'Purines are built on the sugar; pyrimidines are built first and then put on the sugar. Folate feeds the purine ring and thymidine, which is why antifolates stop dividing cells.',
s:[['path','Gout crystals (monosodium urate) are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent: yellow when parallel to the compensator axis. Most gout comes from under-excretion (kidney disease, thiazide and loop diuretics, low-dose aspirin, alcohol); a minority from overproduction (tumour lysis, myeloproliferative disease, Lesch-Nyhan).'],
['bio','Two carbamoyl phosphate synthetases: CPS I in mitochondria uses ammonia for urea and needs N-acetylglutamate; CPS II in the cytoplasm uses glutamine for pyrimidines.'],
['micro','Sulfonamides block bacterial dihydropteroate synthase, upstream of DHFR. Humans take folate from food and have no such enzyme, so sulfonamides spare human cells.'],
['pharm','Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) turns uric acid into soluble allantoin in tumour lysis. It produces hydrogen peroxide, so it causes haemolysis and methaemoglobinaemia in G6PD deficiency.']]},

{h:'The stored fire: glycogen',p:[
'Glycogen is glucose in branched chains (α-1,4 links, an α-1,6 branch every eight to ten residues), built by **glycogen synthase** (rate-limiting) from UDP-glucose and **branching enzyme**. **Glycogen phosphorylase** (rate-limiting for breakdown, with pyridoxal phosphate) snips glucose-1-phosphate from the chain ends until four residues remain before a branch; **debranching enzyme** moves three of them to another chain and frees the last as glucose.',
'Liver and muscle store glycogen for different owners. The liver has **glucose-6-phosphatase** and can release glucose into the blood; muscle lacks it and keeps its glucose for its own glycolysis. That one difference predicts every glycogen storage disease (the full table is in the Halls of Inheritance, Sealed stores): liver defects cause **fasting hypoglycaemia** and a large liver; muscle defects, such as **McArdle disease** (muscle phosphorylase), cause **cramps and myoglobinuria with exercise** and a normal blood glucose; and loss of the lysosomal enzyme, **acid α-glucosidase**, is Pompe disease of heart and muscle.'],
table:{cols:['Signal','Phosphorylase','Synthase','Where'],rows:[
['Glucagon (cAMP, protein kinase A)','on','off','liver only; muscle has no glucagon receptors'],
['Adrenaline at β receptors (cAMP, protein kinase A)','on','off','liver and muscle'],
['Ca²⁺ released with contraction','on (through phosphorylase kinase)','—','muscle'],
['AMP','on (allosteric)','—','muscle'],
['Insulin (protein phosphatase 1)','off','on','liver and muscle'],
['Glucose (liver), glucose-6-phosphate','off','on (allosteric)','liver and muscle']]},
key:'Liver glycogen disease starves the blood; muscle glycogen disease starves the muscle; Pompe, in the lysosome, swells the heart.',
s:[['bio','Why von Gierke disease (glucose-6-phosphatase; see the Furnaces and the Halls of Inheritance) piles up lactate and urate: glucose-6-phosphate that cannot leave the liver floods glycolysis and makes lactate, lactate competes with urate for renal excretion, and trapped phosphate depletes ATP and speeds purine breakdown.'],
['path','McArdle disease: cramps within minutes of intense exercise, a high CK, myoglobinuria, and a "second wind" once blood glucose and fatty acids reach the muscle; on forearm exercise testing venous lactate fails to rise while ammonia rises. Von Gierke type Ib (a glucose-6-phosphate translocase defect) adds neutropenia and inflammatory bowel disease, and long-standing type I grows hepatic adenomas.'],
['phys','Muscle glycogen phosphorylase holds a large share of the body\'s vitamin B6 as its pyridoxal phosphate cofactor.']]},

{h:'Reading the locked rooms',p:[
'Lysosomes are acid rooms of hydrolases. Each enzyme is tagged with **mannose-6-phosphate** in the Golgi so that it is delivered there; when one enzyme is missing, its substrate is never cleared and the lysosomes swell until brain, liver, spleen, marrow or skeleton fail. The enzyme table is in the Halls of Inheritance; here, learn to read the pattern at the bedside.',
'The **sphingolipidoses** store membrane and myelin lipids and strike the nervous system, with or without a big liver and spleen. The **mucopolysaccharidoses** store dermatan and heparan sulfate of connective tissue: coarse faces, thick airways, stiff joints, cloudy corneas and damaged valves. Most are autosomal recessive; only **Fabry disease** and **Hunter syndrome** are X-linked. Tay-Sachs, Gaucher and Niemann-Pick are commoner in Ashkenazi Jewish families. When the mannose-6-phosphate tag itself cannot be added (I-cell disease, in the Nucleus Vault), every enzyme is lost at once and the child looks like a severe mucopolysaccharidosis.'],
table:{cols:['If you see','Think of','Because'],rows:[
['Cherry-red macula, big liver and spleen, infant regression','Niemann-Pick type A','sphingomyelin fills neurons and macrophages'],
['Cherry-red macula, normal-sized liver and spleen, startle to sound','Tay-Sachs','GM2 ganglioside fills neurons only'],
['Pancytopenia, huge spleen, bone crises, normal intellect','Gaucher type 1','glucocerebroside-laden macrophages crowd spleen and marrow'],
['Burning hands and feet, angiokeratomas, proteinuria in a young man','Fabry','globotriaosylceramide builds up in nerves, skin, vessels and kidney'],
['Infant irritability, stiffness, peripheral neuropathy','Krabbe','psychosine poisons the cells that make myelin'],
['Toddler or older child losing walking and speech, with central and peripheral demyelination','Metachromatic leukodystrophy','sulfatides accumulate in myelin'],
['Coarse face with cloudy corneas','Hurler (MPS I)','dermatan and heparan sulfate in cornea and connective tissue'],
['Coarse face with clear corneas in a boy','Hunter (MPS II)','the X-linked mucopolysaccharidosis'],
['Floppy infant with a large heart and tongue','Pompe (acid α-glucosidase)','glycogen trapped in lysosomes of heart and muscle'],
['Very high plasma lysosomal enzymes','I-cell disease','enzymes secreted instead of delivered']]},
key:'Cherry-red macula with a big liver and spleen: Niemann-Pick. Cherry-red macula with a normal abdomen: Tay-Sachs. A coarse-faced boy with clear corneas: Hunter rather than Hurler.',
s:[['pharm','Treatments: enzyme replacement (imiglucerase for Gaucher, agalsidase for Fabry, laronidase for Hurler, alglucosidase alfa for Pompe), substrate reduction (eliglustat, miglustat), a pharmacological chaperone (migalastat for amenable Fabry variants) and stem cell transplantation for Hurler before the brain is damaged.'],
['bio','The cherry-red spot appears because retinal ganglion cells around the macula fill with lipid and turn pale, while the fovea, which has no ganglion cells, still shows the red choroid beneath.'],
['path','Carrying one GBA (glucocerebrosidase) mutation raises the risk of Parkinson disease several-fold; it is the commonest known genetic risk factor for it.'],
['phys','Stored mucopolysaccharide thickens the tongue, airway and neck in Hurler and Hunter syndromes, so sedation and intubation are hazardous.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Two days after starting primaquine for Plasmodium vivax, a man from the Mediterranean coast passes dark urine and becomes jaundiced. His smear shows bite cells. Which product can his red cells not make in sufficient amounts?',o:['ATP','NADPH','2,3-bisphosphoglycerate','Haem'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'g6pd',x:'G6PD is the rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway, the red cell\'s only source of NADPH; without it glutathione stays oxidised and primaquine\'s oxidant stress turns haemoglobin into Heinz bodies. ATP shortage is the problem in pyruvate kinase deficiency, which gives a chronic haemolysis without drug triggers.'},
{q:'A 14-month-old has not eaten for 18 hours because of gastroenteritis. She becomes floppy and has a seizure. Glucose is 1.8 mmol/L (32 mg/dL), urine ketones are absent and plasma octanoylcarnitine (C8) is high. Which process is defective?',o:['Hepatic glycogenolysis','Glucose release by glucose-6-phosphatase','Lysosomal glycogen breakdown','Mitochondrial β-oxidation of fatty acids'],a:3,d:'bio',c:'fatty-acids',x:'MCAD deficiency blocks β-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, so a fasting child can make neither ketones nor enough glucose: hypoketotic hypoglycaemia with raised C8 acylcarnitine. Von Gierke disease (glucose-6-phosphatase) also causes fasting hypoglycaemia, but with lactic acidosis, a large liver and normal acylcarnitines; lysosomal glycogen breakdown (Pompe) does not affect blood glucose.'},
{q:'A 3-day-old boy becomes drowsy and breathes fast a day after milk feeds start. Plasma ammonia is very high, blood urea is low, the gas shows a respiratory alkalosis and urinary orotic acid is raised. Which enzyme is deficient?',o:['Ornithine transcarbamylase','Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I','UMP synthase','Arginase'],a:0,d:'bio',c:'ammonia',x:'OTC deficiency is X-linked and the commonest urea cycle defect; carbamoyl phosphate that cannot join ornithine spills into pyrimidine synthesis and becomes orotic acid. CPS I deficiency gives the same hyperammonaemia without orotic aciduria, and UMP synthase deficiency raises orotic acid with a normal ammonia and megaloblastic anaemia.'},
{q:'A 3-year-old boy with dystonia and delayed development bites his lips and fingertips. His nappies have contained orange, sand-like crystals since infancy, and serum uric acid is high. Which enzyme is missing?',o:['Adenosine deaminase','Xanthine oxidase','HGPRT','Glutamine-PRPP amidotransferase'],a:2,d:'bio',c:'nucleotides',x:'Lesch-Nyhan syndrome is X-linked absence of HGPRT: hypoxanthine and guanine cannot be salvaged, PRPP builds up, de novo purine synthesis accelerates and uric acid soars. Xanthine oxidase deficiency would lower uric acid, and ADA deficiency causes severe combined immunodeficiency rather than hyperuricaemia.'},
{q:'A kidney transplant recipient takes a drug that inhibits IMP dehydrogenase. Lymphocytes are hit hardest because they depend on de novo purine synthesis. Which drug is it?',o:['Azathioprine','Mycophenolate','Methotrexate','Leflunomide'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'nucleotides',x:'Mycophenolate blocks IMP dehydrogenase, the step from IMP to GMP, and lymphocytes have little salvage capacity, so they run short of guanine nucleotides. Azathioprine is a purine analogue converted to 6-mercaptopurine, methotrexate blocks DHFR, and leflunomide blocks dihydroorotate dehydrogenase in pyrimidine synthesis.'},
{q:'A 20-year-old gets painful cramps within minutes of sprinting and passes dark urine afterwards. If he slows down and keeps going, he feels better. Fasting blood glucose is normal. Which enzyme is deficient?',o:['Liver glucose-6-phosphatase','Lysosomal acid α-glucosidase','Glycogen debranching enzyme','Muscle glycogen phosphorylase'],a:3,d:'bio',c:'glycogen-storage',x:'In McArdle disease (type V) muscle cannot release glucose-1-phosphate from glycogen, so intense exercise exhausts the fibres, they break down and myoglobin colours the urine; the "second wind" comes as blood glucose and fatty acids reach the muscle. Glucose-6-phosphatase and debranching defects are mainly liver diseases with fasting hypoglycaemia, and acid α-glucosidase deficiency (Pompe) is a lysosomal myopathy and cardiomyopathy.'},
{q:'A 7-month-old of Ashkenazi Jewish descent has lost head control and startles violently at every sound. Both maculae show a cherry-red spot; the liver and spleen are not enlarged. What is accumulating in his neurons?',o:['GM2 ganglioside','Sphingomyelin','Glucocerebroside','Dermatan sulfate'],a:0,d:'bio',c:'lysosomal',x:'Tay-Sachs disease is hexosaminidase A deficiency, so GM2 ganglioside fills neuronal lysosomes; a cherry-red spot and hyperacusis without organomegaly is its pattern. Niemann-Pick disease (sphingomyelin) also gives a cherry-red spot, but with a large liver and spleen, and Gaucher disease (glucocerebroside) mainly affects spleen, marrow and bone.'}
]};

BOOK['c5-drugs']={depth:5,title:'The Apothecary of Knives',
epi:'Every blade on these shelves was forged against one wall, one ribosome, one enzyme. The things in the Wastes have learned to turn most of them.',
c:['antibiotics','cell-wall-drugs','ribosome-drugs','dna-folate-drugs','antimicrobial-resistance','antimycobacterials','fungi','antivirals'],
S:[
{h:'Blades against the wall',fig:'gram',p:[
'Bacteria wrap themselves in **peptidoglycan**, sugar chains cross-linked by short peptides; human cells have nothing like it, so wall drugs are among the safest. The final cross-link is made by transpeptidases, the **penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)**. **β-lactams** mimic the D-Ala-D-Ala end of the peptide and bind PBPs irreversibly; the wall weakens, autolysins keep cutting, and the growing cell bursts. They are bactericidal and **time-dependent**: what matters is how long the level stays above the MIC. **Vancomycin** blocks the same step from the other side: it binds the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus itself, so the brick cannot be laid. It is too large to cross the Gram-negative outer membrane and covers Gram-positive organisms only, including MRSA.',
'Each family of β-lactam widens the reach of the last. Penicillin covers streptococci, meningococci and Treponema; bulky side chains (flucloxacillin, nafcillin) resist staphylococcal penicillinase; aminopenicillins add Listeria and enterococci; piperacillin adds Pseudomonas. β-lactamase inhibitors (clavulanate, tazobactam, avibactam) shield their partner. Cephalosporins trade Gram-positive for Gram-negative cover as generations climb, until ceftaroline binds the MRSA transpeptidase. No cephalosporin reliably covers Listeria, enterococci or atypical organisms.'],
table:{cols:['Class','Examples','Main use','Watch for'],rows:[
['Natural penicillins','penicillin G, V; benzathine penicillin','streptococci, syphilis, meningococcus','anaphylaxis; Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction when treating syphilis'],
['Antistaphylococcal','flucloxacillin, nafcillin, oxacillin','MSSA','interstitial nephritis; cholestasis (flucloxacillin)'],
['Aminopenicillins ± clavulanate','amoxicillin, ampicillin','otitis, sinusitis, Listeria, enterococci','rash in EBV infection; diarrhoea, C. difficile'],
['Antipseudomonal penicillin','piperacillin-tazobactam','hospital Gram-negatives, Pseudomonas, anaerobes','kidney injury, especially with vancomycin'],
['Cephalosporins, early','cefazolin, cefalexin; cefuroxime, cefoxitin','surgical prophylaxis, skin, UTI; cefoxitin adds anaerobes','cefotetan: bleeding, disulfiram-like reaction'],
['Cephalosporins, late','ceftriaxone, ceftazidime, cefepime, ceftaroline','meningitis, gonorrhoea; Pseudomonas (ceftazidime, cefepime); MRSA (ceftaroline)','ceftriaxone: biliary sludge, displaces bilirubin in neonates; cefepime neurotoxicity in kidney failure'],
['Carbapenems','meropenem, imipenem-cilastatin, ertapenem','ESBL producers, severe mixed infection; ertapenem misses Pseudomonas','seizures (imipenem most); C. difficile'],
['Monobactam','aztreonam','aerobic Gram-negative rods only','no cross-allergy with penicillins (ceftazidime shares a side chain)'],
['Glycopeptide','vancomycin','MRSA; oral for C. difficile','nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, flushing infusion reaction (histamine release), DRESS'],
['Lipopeptide','daptomycin','MRSA and VRE bacteraemia, endocarditis','myopathy with raised CK; inactivated by surfactant, so useless in pneumonia']]},
key:'β-lactams jam the enzyme that cross-links the wall; vancomycin hides the brick the enzyme needs. Both stop the same step from opposite sides.',
s:[['micro','MRSA carries **mecA**, which encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase with low affinity for almost every β-lactam. Penicillin-resistant pneumococci and gonococci alter their PBPs too. Enterococci resist all cephalosporins intrinsically.'],
['pharm','Penicillin allergy is over-reported: most labelled patients tolerate penicillin when tested. Cross-reactivity with cephalosporins is low (about 1–2%) and follows shared side chains; carbapenems are usually tolerated. Probenecid blocks tubular secretion and raises penicillin levels.'],
['path','Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis: fever, rash, eosinophilia, white cell casts and a rising creatinine one to two weeks after starting a β-lactam, sulfonamide, rifampin, NSAID or proton pump inhibitor. Stop the drug.'],
['bio','Cefotetan and cefoperazone carry an N-methylthiotetrazole side chain that blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase (low prothrombin, bleeding) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol).']]},

{h:'Blades against the ribosome',p:[
'Bacterial ribosomes are 70S, built from a **30S** and a **50S** subunit; human cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S. Human mitochondrial ribosomes still look bacterial, and several toxicities come from that resemblance: chloramphenicol marrow suppression, linezolid neuropathy and lactic acidosis, aminoglycoside deafness.',
'On the 30S subunit, **aminoglycosides** (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) block the initiation complex and make the ribosome misread mRNA. They are bactericidal and **concentration-dependent**, and growth stays suppressed after the level falls (the post-antibiotic effect), so they are given once daily. Their uptake needs oxygen, so they do nothing to anaerobes, and a wall drug opens the way in (synergy in enterococcal endocarditis). **Tetracyclines** stop aminoacyl-tRNA reaching the A site; they are bacteriostatic and reach intracellular organisms: Rickettsia, Chlamydia, Mycoplasma and Borrelia.',
'On the 50S subunit, **macrolides** and **clindamycin** block translocation, **chloramphenicol** blocks peptidyl transferase, and **linezolid** prevents the initiation complex forming. Clindamycin covers Gram-positive cocci and anaerobes above the diaphragm, and it switches off toxin production in necrotising fasciitis and toxic shock.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Subunit','Signature toxicity','Tips'],rows:[
['Aminoglycosides','30S','acute tubular necrosis; cochlear and vestibular damage; neuromuscular blockade; fetal deafness','worse with loop diuretics, vancomycin, ciclosporin; monitor levels'],
['Tetracyclines','30S','tooth staining and slowed bone growth under 8 years; photosensitivity; pill oesophagitis','chelated by milk, antacids, iron; doxycycline needs no renal dose change'],
['Macrolides','50S','QT prolongation; cramps and diarrhoea (motilin agonist); cholestatic hepatitis; pyloric stenosis in neonates','erythromycin and clarithromycin inhibit CYP3A4; azithromycin hardly'],
['Clindamycin','50S','C. difficile colitis','aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess; toxin suppression'],
['Chloramphenicol','50S','dose-related marrow suppression; idiosyncratic aplastic anaemia; grey baby syndrome','neonates cannot glucuronidate it'],
['Linezolid','50S','thrombocytopenia with long courses; optic and peripheral neuropathy; serotonin syndrome (weak MAO inhibitor); lactic acidosis','MRSA, VRE; care with SSRIs']]},
key:'Only two families bind the small 30S subunit: aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. Every other ribosome blade here cuts at 50S.',
s:[['micro','Methylation of 23S rRNA (erm genes) blocks macrolides, clindamycin and streptogramins at once. A staphylococcus that is erythromycin-resistant but looks clindamycin-sensitive may carry inducible erm: the **D-test** (a D-shaped clindamycin zone flattened beside an erythromycin disc) reveals it, and clindamycin will fail.'],
['phys','Killing patterns set dosing. Aminoglycosides need a high peak relative to the MIC, so one large daily dose works best. β-lactams need time above the MIC, so they are given often or by extended infusion.'],
['pharm','Erythromycin is given deliberately as a prokinetic in gastroparesis and before endoscopy for upper gastrointestinal bleeding, because it stimulates motilin receptors.'],
['path','Grey baby syndrome: unconjugated chloramphenicol builds up in a neonate, causing vomiting, ashen cyanosis, hypotension and circulatory collapse.']]},

{h:'Blades against folate and DNA',p:[
'Bacteria must make their own folate; humans take it from food. **Sulfonamides** (sulfamethoxazole, sulfadiazine) imitate PABA and block **dihydropteroate synthase**; **trimethoprim** binds bacterial **dihydrofolate reductase** tens of thousands of times more tightly than the human enzyme. Together, as **TMP-SMX**, they block two steps of one road and become bactericidal: urinary infection, Pneumocystis treatment and prophylaxis, Toxoplasma prophylaxis, Nocardia and community MRSA skin infection.',
'**Fluoroquinolones** (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin) inhibit **DNA gyrase** (topoisomerase II, the main target in Gram-negatives) and **topoisomerase IV** (the main target in Gram-positives). Ciprofloxacin is strongest against Gram-negative rods including Pseudomonas; levofloxacin and moxifloxacin add pneumococcus and atypicals. **Metronidazole** is a prodrug: only anaerobes and some protozoa, with their low-redox electron carriers, reduce it to the radical that shreds their DNA, which is why aerobes ignore it. **Nitrofurantoin** concentrates in urine; it treats cystitis but not pyelonephritis, because tissue levels stay low.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Watch for'],rows:[
['Sulfonamides','dihydropteroate synthase','rash, Stevens-Johnson syndrome; haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; kernicterus in neonates; interstitial nephritis, crystalluria; raise warfarin effect'],
['Trimethoprim','bacterial DHFR','hyperkalaemia (blocks ENaC, as amiloride does); creatinine rise without a true fall in GFR (blocks secretion); megaloblastic anaemia if folate is low'],
['Fluoroquinolones','DNA gyrase, topoisomerase IV','tendinopathy and Achilles rupture (older age, steroids); QT prolongation; aortic aneurysm and dissection; dysglycaemia; confusion and seizures; chelated by cations'],
['Metronidazole','DNA, after reduction','disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; metallic taste; neuropathy on long courses; covers anaerobes, Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas, Gardnerella'],
['Nitrofurantoin','DNA and ribosomes (reactive intermediates)','acute pneumonitis and chronic pulmonary fibrosis; neuropathy; haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; avoid in low GFR and at term'],
['Rifampin','DNA-dependent RNA polymerase','see the acid-fast campaign; also used for meningococcal contact prophylaxis']]},
key:'Two locks on one folate road: sulfonamide first, trimethoprim second. Human cells lack the first enzyme and barely bind the drug at the second.',
s:[['pharm','TMP-SMX can push potassium dangerously high in older patients taking ACE inhibitors, ARBs or spironolactone; check potassium within a week of starting.'],
['micro','Fluoroquinolone resistance comes mainly from point mutations in gyrA and parC, helped by efflux pumps and plasmid qnr genes that shield the target.'],
['bio','PABA is the bacterial building block that sulfonamides imitate. Ester local anaesthetics such as procaine break down to PABA and can blunt sulfonamide action.']]},

{h:'How the enemy turns the blade',p:[
'Resistance follows a few routes. **Destroy the drug**: β-lactamases open the ring; extended-spectrum β-lactamases (ESBLs) reach third-generation cephalosporins, and carbapenemases (KPC, NDM, OXA-48) reach carbapenems too. **Change the target**: PBP2a in MRSA, D-Ala-D-**Lac** in vancomycin-resistant enterococci, methylated ribosomes, mutated gyrase, mutated RNA polymerase. **Keep it out**: Pseudomonas loses a porin and imipenem cannot enter. **Pump it out**: efflux pumps export tetracyclines, macrolides and fluoroquinolones.',
'The genes travel. **Plasmids** pass between cells by **conjugation** through a sex pilus and can carry many resistance genes at once; **transposons** jump between plasmid and chromosome; **transformation** takes up naked DNA from dead neighbours; **transduction** moves genes inside phages. Every course of antibiotics selects whichever bacteria already carry these genes, which is the case for narrow, short, well-aimed treatment.'],
table:{cols:['Mechanism','Examples'],rows:[
['Enzymatic inactivation','β-lactamases, ESBLs, carbapenemases; aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes (acetylation, phosphorylation, adenylation); chloramphenicol acetyltransferase'],
['Altered target','mecA/PBP2a (MRSA); altered PBPs (pneumococcus, gonococcus); vanA D-Ala-D-Lac (VRE); erm 23S methylation; gyrA and parC; rpoB (rifampin); katG and inhA (isoniazid)'],
['Reduced entry','porin loss (OprD in Pseudomonas: carbapenems); anaerobes do not take up aminoglycosides'],
['Efflux','tet pumps (tetracyclines), mef (macrolides), multidrug pumps (fluoroquinolones)'],
['Bypass','extra drug-insensitive DHFR or dihydropteroate synthase (TMP-SMX)']]},
key:'Four ways to survive a blade: break it, change what it cuts, bar the door, or throw it back out.',
s:[['micro','Inducible AmpC β-lactamase in Enterobacter, Citrobacter and Serratia can switch on during treatment with a third-generation cephalosporin, so an organism that tested sensitive becomes resistant; cefepime or a carbapenem is preferred for serious infection.'],
['pharm','Newer pairs target specific enzymes: ceftazidime-avibactam and meropenem-vaborbactam inhibit KPC; aztreonam-avibactam covers NDM producers, because aztreonam resists metallo-β-lactamases and avibactam protects it from the others.'],
['path','C. difficile follows any antibiotic that clears the colonic flora; clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins and carbapenems carry the highest risk. Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin treats it.']]},

{h:'The acid-fast campaign',p:[
'Mycobacteria grow slowly, hide in macrophages and caseous necrosis, and wear a waxy wall of **mycolic acids**. A large cavity already contains mutants resistant to any single drug, so tuberculosis is always treated with **combinations**. Drug-sensitive pulmonary TB: **rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol** for 2 months, then rifampin and isoniazid for 4 more; ethambutol stops once susceptibility is confirmed. A 4-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen suits many patients aged 12 and over.',
'**Isoniazid** is a prodrug switched on by the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase **KatG**; it then blocks mycolic acid synthesis. **Rifampin** binds the β subunit of bacterial RNA polymerase. **Pyrazinamide** works best in the acidic phagolysosomes where dormant bacilli shelter. **Ethambutol** blocks arabinosyltransferase. Resistance to both isoniazid and rifampin defines multidrug-resistant TB, now treated with all-oral regimens built on **bedaquiline**, pretomanid and linezolid. **Mycobacterium avium complex** needs a macrolide plus ethambutol; **leprosy** is treated with dapsone and rifampin, adding clofazimine for multibacillary disease.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Signature toxicity','Resistance'],rows:[
['Isoniazid','mycolic acid synthesis (InhA), after KatG activation','hepatitis (age, alcohol); neuropathy and sideroblastic anaemia from B6 depletion; drug-induced lupus; seizures in overdose (give pyridoxine)','katG or inhA mutation'],
['Rifampin','RNA polymerase β subunit','orange-red urine, tears and sweat; hepatitis; potent CYP inducer; flu-like illness and thrombocytopenia with intermittent dosing','rpoB mutation, rapid if used alone'],
['Pyrazinamide','pyrazinoic acid in acidic phagolysosomes','hepatotoxicity; hyperuricaemia and gout; arthralgia','pncA mutation'],
['Ethambutol','arabinosyltransferase','optic neuritis: falling acuity, red-green colour loss; reduce dose in kidney failure','embB mutation'],
['Bedaquiline','mycobacterial ATP synthase','QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity','atpE mutation, efflux'],
['Dapsone','dihydropteroate synthase','haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, methaemoglobinaemia, agranulocytosis','folP mutation']]},
key:'Three of the four first-line drugs can injure the liver; only ethambutol aims at the eye. Pyridoxine goes with isoniazid.',
s:[['pharm','Rifampin speeds the clearance of warfarin, oral contraceptives, ciclosporin, azoles, methadone and HIV protease inhibitors. With a boosted protease inhibitor, rifabutin, a weaker inducer, takes its place.'],
['bio','Isoniazid binds pyridoxine and blocks its activation. Pyridoxal phosphate is the cofactor of δ-aminolaevulinate synthase (hence sideroblastic anaemia) and of glutamate decarboxylase, which makes GABA (hence seizures in overdose).'],
['micro','Cord factor (trehalose dimycolate) and sulfatides are the wall\'s virulence factors; the same mycolic acids hold carbol fuchsin through acid-alcohol, which is what acid-fast means.'],
['path','Latent TB (positive interferon-γ release assay, clear chest film) is treated to prevent reactivation: weekly isoniazid with rifapentine for 3 months, rifampin for 4 months, isoniazid with rifampin for 3 months, or isoniazid for 6–9 months.']]},

{h:'Against moulds and yeasts',p:[
'Fungi are eukaryotes, and their ribosomes and enzymes resemble ours. Antifungals exploit two differences: the fungal membrane uses **ergosterol** where ours uses cholesterol, and the fungal wall is built of **β-glucan** and chitin, which we lack.',
'**Amphotericin B** binds ergosterol and opens pores that leak K⁺ and Mg²⁺. It is fungicidal and kept for the gravest infections (cryptococcal meningitis with flucytosine, mucormycosis, severe endemic mycoses), but it also binds cholesterol, and the kidney pays. **Azoles** inhibit the fungal cytochrome P450 **lanosterol 14α-demethylase**, so ergosterol is never made. **Echinocandins** inhibit **β-(1,3)-glucan synthase** and are first-line for candidaemia. **Flucytosine** is turned by fungal cytosine deaminase into 5-fluorouracil and is used only in combination. **Terbinafine** blocks squalene epoxidase and concentrates in keratin; **griseofulvin** disrupts the microtubules of dividing fungi in hair and skin.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Target','Best use','Watch for'],rows:[
['Amphotericin B','ergosterol (pores)','cryptococcal meningitis, mucormycosis, severe histoplasmosis','nephrotoxicity with hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia; fever and rigors on infusion; anaemia; liposomal form and saline loading protect the kidney'],
['Nystatin','ergosterol','oral thrush, topical candidiasis','too toxic for systemic use'],
['Fluconazole','14α-demethylase','candidiasis; cryptococcal maintenance; reaches CSF','misses Aspergillus, Mucor and Candida krusei; often fails C. glabrata'],
['Itraconazole','14α-demethylase','histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, sporotrichosis','negative inotrope (avoid in heart failure); erratic absorption'],
['Voriconazole','14α-demethylase','invasive aspergillosis','visual disturbance, hallucinations; photosensitivity and skin cancer with long use; periostitis'],
['Posaconazole, isavuconazole','14α-demethylase','mucormycosis; prophylaxis in neutropenia','isavuconazole shortens rather than lengthens QT'],
['Echinocandins','β-(1,3)-glucan synthase','candidaemia; salvage for Aspergillus','IV only; flushing; poor CSF and urine levels'],
['Flucytosine','DNA and RNA (as 5-FU)','with amphotericin for cryptococcal meningitis','marrow suppression'],
['Terbinafine','squalene epoxidase','dermatophytes, onychomycosis','hepatotoxicity, taste disturbance'],
['Griseofulvin','microtubules','tinea capitis','teratogen, CYP inducer, disulfiram-like reaction']]},
key:'Polyenes punch holes in the ergosterol, azoles stop it being made, echinocandins attack the glucan wall. Pick the difference, then the drug.',
s:[['pharm','Every azole inhibits human cytochrome P450 to some degree (ketoconazole and voriconazole most): warfarin, ciclosporin, tacrolimus and statin levels rise, and most azoles prolong QT. Ketoconazole also blocks adrenal and gonadal steroid synthesis, causing gynaecomastia; it is sometimes used to treat Cushing syndrome.'],
['phys','Amphotericin pores in the collecting duct let H⁺ leak back and K⁺ leak out: a distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis with hypokalaemia. Replace potassium and magnesium daily.'],
['micro','Candida auris is a multidrug-resistant yeast that spreads between hospital patients and survives on surfaces; echinocandins are the usual first choice.']]},

{h:'Against the hijackers: antivirals',p:[
'Viruses borrow our machinery, so antivirals must hit steps that belong to the virus alone: a viral kinase, polymerase, protease or release enzyme. **Aciclovir** and its relatives are guanosine analogues switched on by the **viral thymidine kinase**, so they are activated mainly in infected cells; the triphosphate then stops viral DNA polymerase. They cannot reach latent virus. **Ganciclovir** depends on the CMV kinase **UL97** instead. **Foscarnet** and **cidofovir** need no viral kinase at all, so they still work when the kinase mutates, at a cost to the kidney.',
'For **influenza**, oseltamivir and zanamivir stop neuraminidase releasing new virions, and baloxavir blocks the viral cap-snatching endonuclease. **Hepatitis B** is suppressed with tenofovir or entecavir. **Hepatitis C** is now cured in most patients by 8–12 weeks of direct-acting antivirals; test for hepatitis B first, because it can reactivate as HCV clears.',
'**HIV** drug classes and their toxicities are set out in the Empty Barracks (Holding the virus back). The principles: at least two fully active drugs from different classes, usually two nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors with an integrase inhibitor; an undetectable viral load, which means the virus is untransmittable; and pre-exposure prophylaxis with oral tenofovir and emtricitabine or long-acting cabotegravir or lenacapavir injections. The dangerous meeting points with this chapter are interactions: boosted protease inhibitors with rifampin, azoles and macrolides.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Used for','Activation and target','Watch for'],rows:[
['Aciclovir, valaciclovir, famciclovir','HSV, VZV','viral thymidine kinase, then viral DNA polymerase','crystal nephropathy at high IV doses (hydrate); confusion in kidney failure; resistance through kinase mutation'],
['Ganciclovir, valganciclovir','CMV','CMV UL97 kinase, then DNA polymerase','neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; teratogen'],
['Foscarnet','aciclovir-resistant HSV; ganciclovir-resistant CMV','binds DNA polymerase directly (pyrophosphate analogue)','nephrotoxicity; low ionised calcium and magnesium; seizures'],
['Cidofovir','resistant CMV','already a nucleotide; DNA polymerase','nephrotoxicity (give with probenecid and saline); neutropenia'],
['Letermovir','CMV prophylaxis after transplant','CMV terminase complex','spares the marrow; CYP interactions'],
['Oseltamivir, zanamivir','influenza A and B','neuraminidase (release of new virions)','nausea; inhaled zanamivir can trigger bronchospasm'],
['Baloxavir','influenza A and B','cap-dependent endonuclease of the viral polymerase','single dose; resistance can emerge'],
['Tenofovir, entecavir','hepatitis B','reverse transcriptase','tenofovir disoproxil: kidney tubule and bone; hepatitis flare if stopped'],
['Sofosbuvir with an NS5A inhibitor (-asvir)','hepatitis C, all genotypes','NS5B polymerase and the NS5A replication complex','hepatitis B reactivation; severe bradycardia with amiodarone (sofosbuvir)'],
['Ribavirin','severe RSV in selected patients; older hepatitis C regimens','guanosine analogue; inhibits IMP dehydrogenase','haemolytic anaemia; strongly teratogenic']]},
key:'Aciclovir waits for the virus\'s own kinase to wake it; foscarnet needs no kinase at all. That is why foscarnet still works when the kinase mutates.',
s:[['bio','Nucleoside analogues for HIV and hepatitis B lack the 3′-hydroxyl group needed to form the next phosphodiester bond, so the DNA chain stops. Host kinases must phosphorylate them first; tenofovir, already a nucleotide, needs one step fewer.'],
['pharm','Ritonavir and cobicistat are given in small doses to block CYP3A4, not to fight HIV, so the partner protease inhibitor stays high. The same block raises inhaled fluticasone (iatrogenic Cushing syndrome), several statins and many other drugs.'],
['micro','Tenofovir, emtricitabine and lamivudine also suppress hepatitis B. Stopping them in a co-infected patient can trigger a severe hepatitis B flare.'],
['phys','Foscarnet chelates ionised calcium and magnesium: perioral tingling, tetany, arrhythmia and seizures can occur while the total calcium looks normal. Check ionised calcium, magnesium and creatinine.'],
['path','CMV retinitis (CD4 below 50) is a spreading white, granular retinal necrosis with haemorrhage along the vessels; untreated, it blinds. Valganciclovir or ganciclovir treats it, with foscarnet or cidofovir for resistance.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'An Enterococcus faecium line infection keeps growing despite vancomycin. Which change in the organism explains it?',o:['Production of the low-affinity PBP2a','D-Ala-D-Ala replaced by D-Ala-D-Lac','Loss of an outer-membrane porin','Methylation of its 23S ribosomal RNA'],a:1,d:'micro',c:'cell-wall-drugs',x:'Vancomycin binds the D-Ala-D-Ala end of cell wall precursors; vanA and related genes swap the last residue for lactate, and the drug can no longer bind tightly. PBP2a is the MRSA mechanism against β-lactams, and enterococci have no outer membrane, so porin loss does not apply.'},
{q:'A man with Gram-negative sepsis is treated with gentamicin and furosemide. On day 6 he has tinnitus, unsteadiness and a rising creatinine. Which other statement about his antibiotic is true?',o:['Uptake needs oxygen, so anaerobes resist it','It binds the 50S ribosomal subunit','It strongly inhibits CYP3A4 in the liver','It kills best at a low, steady level'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'ribosome-drugs',x:'Aminoglycosides cross into bacteria by an oxygen-dependent transport step, so anaerobes are intrinsically resistant; their toxicities are ototoxicity and acute tubular necrosis, both worsened by loop diuretics. They bind the 30S subunit and kill in proportion to peak concentration, which is why they are given as one large daily dose rather than a low, steady level.'},
{q:'Two months into treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis, a woman notices blurred vision and can no longer tell red from green. Which drug is responsible, and what does it inhibit?',o:['Isoniazid; mycolic acid synthesis','Rifampin; RNA polymerase','Pyrazinamide; DNA gyrase','Ethambutol; arabinosyltransferase'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'antimycobacterials',x:'Ethambutol blocks arabinosyltransferase and so the arabinogalactan of the mycobacterial wall; its signature toxicity is a dose-related optic neuritis with red-green colour loss, so vision is checked before and during treatment. Isoniazid mainly injures the liver and peripheral nerves, and rifampin turns body fluids orange rather than harming the optic nerve.'},
{q:'A woman taking sertraline starts an oral antibiotic for vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. Two days later she is agitated, sweating and febrile, with inducible clonus. Which antibiotic was given?',o:['Daptomycin','Tigecycline','Linezolid','Nitrofurantoin'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'ribosome-drugs',x:'Linezolid is a weak, reversible MAO inhibitor, so combined with an SSRI it can cause serotonin syndrome: clonus, hyperthermia and agitation. Daptomycin also treats VRE, but it is given IV only and its toxicity is myopathy with a raised CK, not serotonin excess.'},
{q:'CMV retinitis in a man with AIDS progresses on ganciclovir. Sequencing shows a mutation in the viral UL97 kinase. Which drug still works, and what needs close monitoring?',o:['Foscarnet; calcium, magnesium, creatinine','Aciclovir; creatinine and hydration only','Valganciclovir; neutrophil and platelet count','Oseltamivir; no monitoring needed'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'antivirals',x:'Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analogue that binds viral DNA polymerase directly and needs no activation by UL97, so the mutation does not affect it; it is nephrotoxic and chelates calcium and magnesium, which can cause seizures. Valganciclovir is simply the oral prodrug of ganciclovir and depends on the same mutated kinase.'},
{q:'On day 5 of amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis, potassium is 2.9 mmol/L and there is a normal anion gap acidosis with a urine pH above 5.5. What is the mechanism?',o:['Binding of cholesterol in adrenal cells','Leaky collecting duct: distal (type 1) RTA','Inhibition of fungal 14α-demethylase','ENaC blockade, as with amiloride'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'fungi',x:'Amphotericin binds membrane sterols, and in the collecting duct it makes cells leaky, so H⁺ leaks back and K⁺ is wasted: a distal renal tubular acidosis with hypokalaemia and an inappropriately high urine pH. ENaC blockade (amiloride, trimethoprim) raises potassium rather than lowering it, and 14α-demethylase is the azole target.'},
{q:'A man whose HIV is controlled on darunavir boosted with ritonavir is diagnosed with drug-sensitive pulmonary TB. What is the main problem with standard therapy, and the usual solution?',o:['Isoniazid inhibits integrase; use ethambutol alone','Ethambutol blocks HIV protease; use streptomycin','Pyrazinamide raises bilirubin; leave it out','Rifampin induces CYP3A4; use rifabutin instead'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'antimycobacterials',x:'Rifampin strongly induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, so protease inhibitor levels collapse and HIV can become resistant. Rifabutin is a much weaker inducer and is the usual substitute, with its dose adjusted because ritonavir raises its level. A single drug such as ethambutol alone would breed resistant TB.'}
]};

BOOK['c6-sleepers']={depth:6,title:'The Sleeping Draughts',
epi:'Some currents carry nothing but sleep. They switch the Host off, piece by piece, and nearly always let it wake. Learn the ones that do not.',
c:['general-anaesthetics','local-anaesthetics','nmj-blockers','opioids','analgesics','pharmacokinetics'],
S:[
{h:'Breathing the sleep in: inhaled anaesthetics',fig:'drc',p:[
'General anaesthesia is a reversible, drug-induced loss of consciousness with amnesia, immobility and blunted reflexes. The volatile agents (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane) probably work by enhancing inhibitory GABA-A and glycine receptors and opening background K⁺ channels; nitrous oxide mainly blocks NMDA receptors. **Potency** is measured as the **minimum alveolar concentration (MAC)**: the alveolar concentration, at one atmosphere, that stops movement to a surgical incision in half of patients. It is the ED50 of anaesthesia (see the dose-response figure). Potency = 1/MAC, and it rises with **lipid solubility**.',
'**Speed** depends on a different property, the **blood:gas partition coefficient**. A poorly soluble agent saturates the blood quickly, so its partial pressure in alveoli, blood and brain rises fast: fast sleep and fast waking. A soluble agent is soaked up by the blood and the brain fills slowly. A high cardiac output carries more agent away from the alveoli and slows induction.',
'All volatile agents lower blood pressure, depress breathing and blunt the response to CO₂, dilate cerebral vessels (cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure rise as brain metabolism falls), relax bronchi and uterus, and deepen neuromuscular block. Sevoflurane is sweet and non-irritant, so children are put to sleep with it by mask; desflurane is pungent.'],
table:{cols:['Agent','Blood:gas','MAC (%)','Notes'],rows:[
['Nitrous oxide','0.47','about 104','too weak to anaesthetise alone; expands air spaces; inactivates B12; no malignant hyperthermia'],
['Desflurane','0.42','about 6','fastest waking; airway irritant; large climate footprint'],
['Sevoflurane','0.65','about 2','smooth mask induction; bronchodilator'],
['Isoflurane','1.4','about 1.2','slower; strong vasodilator'],
['Halothane','2.4','about 0.75','slow; sensitises the heart to catecholamines; halothane hepatitis']]},
key:'Potency follows fat (lipid solubility, low MAC). Speed follows blood (low blood solubility, fast in and out). Never swap the two.',
s:[['path','**Malignant hyperthermia**: an autosomal dominant defect, usually in the ryanodine receptor RYR1, lets volatile agents or succinylcholine release Ca²⁺ uncontrollably from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Rising end-tidal CO₂ and tachycardia come first, then masseter and generalised rigidity, hyperkalaemia, acidosis, rhabdomyolysis and late hyperthermia. Stop the trigger, cool, and give **dantrolene**, which blocks RYR1.'],
['bio','Nitrous oxide oxidises the cobalt of vitamin B12 and inactivates methionine synthase. Repeated or recreational use causes megaloblastic anaemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord, sometimes with a normal serum B12; homocysteine rises.'],
['phys','MAC falls with age, pregnancy, hypothermia, acute alcohol intoxication and added opioids or benzodiazepines; it rises with chronic alcohol use, hyperthermia, acute stimulant use and in infants.'],
['pharm','Nitrous oxide is about 30 times more soluble in blood than nitrogen, so it enters closed air spaces faster than nitrogen leaves: avoid it with a pneumothorax, bowel obstruction or recent eye surgery with a gas bubble. When it is stopped it floods into the alveoli and dilutes oxygen (diffusion hypoxia), so give 100% oxygen.']]},

{h:'Through the vein: IV agents',p:[
'IV induction agents reach the brain within one arm-to-brain circulation. A single dose wears off by **redistribution**, not elimination: brain and heart receive the drug first, then it moves into muscle and later fat, and the brain level falls below the waking threshold while most of the drug is still in the body. After long infusions the tissues fill up and waking slows (a rising context-sensitive half-time).',
'**Propofol** potentiates GABA-A receptors and is the usual induction and infusion agent: smooth, fast waking and little nausea, but it lowers blood pressure, stops breathing at induction doses and stings on injection. **Thiopental**, a barbiturate, lowers brain metabolism and intracranial pressure and must not be given in acute porphyria. **Ketamine** blocks NMDA receptors: a dissociated state with open eyes and nystagmus, strong analgesia, preserved breathing and airway reflexes, bronchodilation, and a sympathetic surge that holds blood pressure up; emergence hallucinations are its price. **Etomidate** barely touches heart or vessels but inhibits adrenal **11β-hydroxylase**. **Midazolam** sedates and erases memory; **dexmedetomidine**, an α₂ agonist, sedates while leaving the patient rousable and breathing.'],
table:{cols:['Agent','Target','Chosen when','Watch for'],rows:[
['Propofol','GABA-A','routine induction; total IV anaesthesia; ICU sedation','hypotension, apnoea, injection pain; propofol infusion syndrome after long high-dose infusion (acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, high triglycerides, heart failure)'],
['Thiopental','GABA-A (barbiturate)','raised intracranial pressure; refractory status epilepticus','porphyria; hypotension; tissue necrosis if extravasated'],
['Ketamine','NMDA antagonist','shock, trauma, asthma; analgesia; procedural sedation','emergence delirium, hypertension, tachycardia, hypersalivation'],
['Etomidate','GABA-A','haemodynamic instability','adrenal suppression, myoclonus, nausea'],
['Midazolam','GABA-A (benzodiazepine site)','sedation, amnesia, seizures','respiratory depression with opioids; reversed by flumazenil'],
['Dexmedetomidine','α₂ agonist','ICU sedation; awake fibreoptic intubation','bradycardia, hypotension']]},
key:'Ketamine holds the pressure up, propofol lets it fall, and etomidate leaves it alone but quietly borrows the adrenals.',
s:[['phys','Barbiturates lengthen each opening of the GABA-A chloride channel and at high doses open it without GABA; benzodiazepines make openings more frequent and need GABA present. That is why barbiturate overdose has no ceiling and benzodiazepines alone rarely stop breathing.'],
['bio','Etomidate blocks 11β-hydroxylase, the last step to cortisol (the enzyme metyrapone also inhibits); even a single dose suppresses cortisol for many hours.'],
['micro','Propofol is a soybean-oil and egg-lecithin emulsion that supports bacterial growth: draw it up aseptically and discard opened syringes within hours. Most people with egg allergy tolerate it.'],
['pharm','Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines but can provoke seizures in long-term users and in mixed overdoses with tricyclics, so it is not given blindly. Esketamine nasal spray, a ketamine enantiomer, is licensed for treatment-resistant depression.']]},

{h:'Numbing the wire: local anaesthetics',fig:'neuroap',p:[
'Local anaesthetics block **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels** from inside the axon, so no action potential can start or travel (switch the figure to lidocaine). They are weak bases. The **uncharged** form crosses the membrane; inside, it picks up a proton and the **charged** form binds the channel. The block is **use-dependent**: channels that open often are blocked more, so firing pain fibres go quiet first.',
'Small fibres are blocked before large ones, and myelinated before unmyelinated of the same size: pain and temperature go first, then touch and pressure, and motor power last. In **infected tissue** the low pH keeps more drug charged outside the nerve, so less crosses and the block is weak. **Adrenaline** constricts local vessels, so the drug stays longer and less reaches the circulation.',
'Two families are told apart by the link in the middle of the molecule. **Esters** (procaine, chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine, cocaine) are hydrolysed by plasma pseudocholinesterase and release PABA, the cause of most true local anaesthetic allergy. **Amides** (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine) are metabolised by the liver; allergy is rare, and their names carry an i before the -caine.'],
table:{cols:['Agent','Family','Notes'],rows:[
['Lidocaine','amide','fast onset, medium duration; also a class Ib antiarrhythmic; maximum about 3–4.5 mg/kg plain, 7 mg/kg with adrenaline'],
['Bupivacaine','amide','long acting; the most cardiotoxic (binds cardiac Na⁺ channels and leaves slowly); maximum about 2 mg/kg; never for IV regional anaesthesia'],
['Ropivacaine, levobupivacaine','amide','long acting, less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine'],
['Prilocaine','amide','methaemoglobinaemia (o-toluidine metabolite); IV regional anaesthesia; in EMLA cream with lidocaine'],
['Chloroprocaine','ester','very short acting; rapid plasma hydrolysis makes it safe in obstetrics'],
['Benzocaine','ester','topical only; methaemoglobinaemia from throat sprays'],
['Cocaine','ester','topical for nasal surgery; the only one that constricts vessels (blocks noradrenaline reuptake); hypertension, arrhythmia']]},
key:'An acid abscess keeps the drug charged outside the nerve, so it cannot get in: infected tissue numbs badly.',
s:[['pharm','**Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity** follows an overdose or an accidental IV injection: perioral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus and light-headedness, then seizures, then coma and cardiovascular collapse (bradycardia, wide QRS, ventricular arrhythmia), worst with bupivacaine. Support the airway, give a benzodiazepine for seizures and **20% lipid emulsion**; use smaller adrenaline doses in resuscitation and never lidocaine as the antiarrhythmic.'],
['path','Methaemoglobinaemia from prilocaine or benzocaine: cyanosis that oxygen does not relieve, chocolate-brown blood and pulse oximetry stuck near 85%. Treat with methylene blue, unless the patient has G6PD deficiency (the Furnace Ledgers).'],
['phys','In a spinal anaesthetic, small preganglionic sympathetic fibres are blocked several segments above the sensory level, so vasodilation and hypotension are expected; a high block reaching T1–T4 also removes the cardiac accelerator fibres and slows the heart.'],
['bio','Pseudocholinesterase deficiency slows the breakdown of ester local anaesthetics and of succinylcholine; amides are unaffected.']]},

{h:'Cutting the strings: neuromuscular blockers',p:[
'At the neuromuscular junction acetylcholine opens **nicotinic (Nm) receptors** on the motor end plate. Neuromuscular blockers paralyse skeletal muscle for intubation and surgery. They have no sedative or analgesic action: a paralysed patient without anaesthesia is awake, in pain and unable to show it.',
'**Succinylcholine**, two acetylcholine molecules joined, is the only **depolarising** blocker. It opens the receptor and keeps the end plate depolarised (visible fasciculations); the neighbouring Na⁺ channels stay inactivated and the muscle goes flaccid (phase I block). It acts within about a minute and is gone in 5–10 minutes, because **plasma pseudocholinesterase** hydrolyses it; prolonged exposure turns it into a phase II block that behaves like a non-depolarising one. It raises potassium by about 0.5 mmol/L in everyone, and far more where receptors have spread beyond the end plate.',
'**Non-depolarising** blockers (rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium) are competitive antagonists. More acetylcholine overcomes them, so **neostigmine** reverses them, given with **glycopyrrolate** or atropine to block its muscarinic effects. **Sugammadex** encapsulates rocuronium and vecuronium and reverses even deep block within minutes. Atracurium and cisatracurium break down spontaneously in plasma (**Hofmann elimination**), so they suit liver and kidney failure.'],
table:{cols:['Feature','Succinylcholine','Non-depolarising (e.g. rocuronium)'],rows:[
['Action','agonist; persistent depolarisation','competitive antagonist'],
['Fasciculations','yes, then flaccid paralysis','no'],
['Train-of-four','phase I: equal small twitches, no fade; phase II: fade','fade; post-tetanic potentiation'],
['Neostigmine','deepens phase I block','reverses block'],
['Onset and duration','about 60 s; 5–10 min','60–90 s at intubating dose; 30–60 min'],
['Clearance','plasma pseudocholinesterase','liver and kidney (aminosteroids); Hofmann elimination (atracurium, cisatracurium)'],
['Signature risks','hyperkalaemia, malignant hyperthermia, bradycardia, raised intraocular and intragastric pressure, myalgia','anaphylaxis (rocuronium), histamine release (atracurium), tachycardia (pancuronium)']]},
key:'Paralysis is not sleep. Every blocker must sit on top of an anaesthetic, and every paralysed patient must be watched for awareness.',
s:[['path','Avoid succinylcholine where nicotinic receptors have spread over the whole muscle membrane: burns and crush injury after the first day or two, denervation (spinal cord injury, stroke), prolonged immobility and myopathies. A boy with undiagnosed Duchenne dystrophy can have a hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, which is why it is avoided for routine use in children.'],
['bio','Atypical pseudocholinesterase (autosomal recessive; low dibucaine number) turns a 10-minute block into hours of apnoea; keep the patient sedated and ventilated until it wears off. Organophosphate poisoning and severe liver disease prolong it too.'],
['phys','Myasthenia gravis (fewer receptors) resists succinylcholine and is very sensitive to non-depolarising blockers; Lambert-Eaton syndrome (less acetylcholine release) is sensitive to both. Hypothermia, magnesium and volatile anaesthetics deepen non-depolarising block.'],
['micro','Aminoglycosides, polymyxins and clindamycin potentiate neuromuscular blockade, and aminoglycosides can unmask or worsen myasthenia gravis.'],
['pharm','Sugammadex also binds hormonal contraceptives: advise back-up contraception for 7 days. It does not reverse atracurium, cisatracurium or succinylcholine.']]},

{h:'The poppy\'s children: opioids',p:[
'Opioids act on **μ, κ and δ receptors**, all coupled to Gi/o. They lower cAMP, open postsynaptic K⁺ channels and close presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels, so dorsal horn neurons release less glutamate and substance P. μ receptors carry most of the analgesia, euphoria and dependence, and almost all of the danger.',
'The effects follow the receptor map. In the brainstem opioids blunt the response to CO₂: breathing slows and stops, which is how they kill. They constrict the pupils, suppress cough, cause nausea at the chemoreceptor trigger zone, slow the gut, tighten the sphincter of Oddi and the bladder sphincter, and (morphine) release histamine. **Tolerance** develops to analgesia, euphoria, sedation and respiratory depression, but hardly to miosis and constipation.',
'Overdose means coma, slow shallow breathing and pinpoint pupils. **Naloxone** reverses it within minutes but lasts only 30–90 minutes, less than methadone or slow-release opioids, so patients can slip back and may need repeat doses or an infusion. **Naltrexone**, longer acting, helps maintain abstinence from opioids and alcohol once withdrawal is over.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','What sets it apart'],rows:[
['Morphine','reference μ agonist; active morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates in kidney failure; histamine release'],
['Fentanyl','50–100 times as potent as morphine, very lipid-soluble; patches for chronic pain; chest wall rigidity with rapid high doses; illicit fentanyl drives most overdose deaths'],
['Codeine, tramadol','prodrugs activated by CYP2D6: ultra-rapid metabolisers can be poisoned (deaths in children and breastfed infants), poor metabolisers get little relief; tramadol also blocks serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake (seizures, serotonin syndrome)'],
['Meperidine (pethidine)','normeperidine causes seizures, worst in kidney failure; serotonin syndrome with MAO inhibitors'],
['Methadone','long, variable half-life; also an NMDA antagonist; QT prolongation; opioid use disorder and pain'],
['Buprenorphine','partial μ agonist with very high affinity: a ceiling on respiratory depression; precipitates withdrawal if given while a full agonist is on board'],
['Loperamide','μ agonist kept out of the brain by P-glycoprotein; diarrhoea; torsades in massive misuse'],
['Methylnaltrexone, naloxegol','peripheral μ antagonists; treat opioid constipation without loss of analgesia']]},
key:'Tolerance comes to almost everything an opioid does except the small pupil and the slow bowel.',
s:[['phys','Opioid hypoventilation raises PaCO₂ with a normal A–a gradient, and the hypoxaemia corrects with ventilation or naloxone. The retained CO₂ dilates cerebral vessels and raises intracranial pressure, a danger after head injury.'],
['bio','CYP2D6 turns codeine into morphine. Inhibitors such as fluoxetine, paroxetine and bupropion make codeine and tramadol weaker painkillers.'],
['pharm','Opioid withdrawal (dilated pupils, yawning, tears, runny nose, gooseflesh, diarrhoea, cramps) is miserable but rarely dangerous. Methadone or buprenorphine treats it; the α₂ agonists clonidine and lofexidine ease the autonomic storm.'],
['path','Heroin and other opioid overdoses can cause non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, and prolonged immobility on the floor can cause rhabdomyolysis and compartment syndrome.'],
['micro','Injecting drug use brings right-sided (tricuspid) endocarditis, usually Staphylococcus aureus, with septic pulmonary emboli.']]},

{h:'Beyond the poppy: the rest of the pain ledger',p:[
'Good analgesia is **multimodal**: drugs acting at different points are combined, so each can be given at a lower, safer dose and opioids are spared. Prostaglandins sensitise nociceptors at the injury and in the dorsal horn. **Cyclooxygenase** turns arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H₂ and then into prostaglandins, prostacyclin and thromboxane. COX-1 is constitutive (gastric protection, platelet thromboxane, renal blood flow); COX-2 is induced by inflammation and also works constantly in kidney and endothelium.',
'**NSAIDs** (ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac, indometacin, ketorolac) inhibit both enzymes reversibly. **Aspirin** acetylates COX irreversibly, and platelets cannot make more, so one dose impairs them for their 7–10 day lifespan. **Celecoxib** spares COX-1, so it causes fewer ulcers, but it cuts endothelial prostacyclin while platelet thromboxane runs on, raising the risk of thrombosis. **Paracetamol** acts mainly in the CNS: it relieves pain and fever but not inflammation, spares stomach and platelets, and is toxic to the liver only in overdose.',
'**Neuropathic pain** (burning, shooting, allodynia) barely responds to NSAIDs. First choices are **gabapentinoids** (binding the α2δ subunit of voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels), **tricyclics** and **SNRIs** (duloxetine), which strengthen descending inhibition. Carbamazepine is first-line for trigeminal neuralgia.'],
table:{cols:['Pain','First choices','Why'],rows:[
['Mild acute or musculoskeletal','paracetamol, an NSAID, or both','prostaglandin-driven; opioids add little'],
['Severe acute or after surgery','regional block plus paracetamol and NSAID, then a short opioid course','multimodal: lower opioid doses, fewer side effects'],
['Neuropathic','gabapentinoid, tricyclic or SNRI; carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia','ectopic firing of damaged nerves; COX is not the driver'],
['Gout flare','NSAID, colchicine or a steroid','neutrophil-driven inflammation'],
['Migraine attack','NSAID or paracetamol, then a triptan (5-HT1B/1D agonist)','trigeminovascular activation'],
['Cancer pain','regular strong opioid with a laxative, plus adjuvants for bone and nerve pain','titrated to effect; full agonists have no ceiling']]},
key:'Aspirin silences COX for the life of the platelet; every other NSAID lets go when the drug leaves.',
s:[['phys','Prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole and hold up GFR when perfusion falls. An NSAID in a dehydrated patient on an ACE inhibitor and a diuretic removes the dilation in front of the glomerulus while the ACE inhibitor removes the constriction behind it: acute kidney injury.'],
['path','NSAIDs also cause acute interstitial nephritis (sometimes with minimal change nephrotic syndrome) and, after years of heavy use, renal papillary necrosis. Aspirin in a child with influenza or varicella can cause **Reye syndrome**: microvesicular fatty liver, hyperammonaemia and encephalopathy.'],
['pharm','NSAIDs cause peptic ulcers and bleeding (protect with a PPI or misoprostol), fluid retention and hypertension. In aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, blocking COX shunts arachidonic acid to leukotrienes: asthma, nasal polyps and flushing.'],
['bio','Indometacin closes a patent ductus arteriosus in premature infants by cutting prostaglandin E₂; alprostadil (PGE₁) keeps the duct open in duct-dependent heart disease.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Two volatile anaesthetics are equally potent. Which property makes one of them induce anaesthesia and wear off faster?',o:['A higher oil:gas partition coefficient','A lower MAC','A lower blood:gas partition coefficient','A higher cardiac output in the patient'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'general-anaesthetics',x:'Little of a poorly blood-soluble agent dissolves in blood, so alveolar, arterial and brain partial pressures rise and fall quickly. Oil:gas solubility and MAC describe potency, not speed, and a high cardiac output actually slows induction by carrying agent away from the alveoli.'},
{q:'Twenty minutes after induction with sevoflurane and succinylcholine, end-tidal CO₂ climbs despite more ventilation, the heart rate rises and the jaw is rigid. Potassium is 6.4 mmol/L. The specific treatment acts on which structure?',o:['Ryanodine receptor (RYR1) in muscle','Dopamine D₂ receptor in the striatum','Nicotinic receptor of the end plate','Hypothalamic temperature centre'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'general-anaesthetics',x:'Malignant hyperthermia is uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release through a mutant RYR1 channel, so muscle burns ATP, makes CO₂ and heat, and breaks down; dantrolene blocks RYR1. Bromocriptine acts on D₂ receptors and belongs to neuroleptic malignant syndrome, a different condition caused by dopamine blockade.'},
{q:'A man with 40% burns from ten days ago needs emergency intubation. Which drug should be avoided, and why?',o:['Rocuronium; histamine-mediated bronchospasm','Succinylcholine; K⁺ surge from spread receptors','Propofol; suppression of cortisol synthesis','Ketamine; loss of airway reflexes and apnoea'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'nmj-blockers',x:'After burns, denervation or immobility, nicotinic receptors spread across the whole muscle membrane; succinylcholine opens all of them at once and potassium can surge to arrest levels. High-dose rocuronium is the usual alternative for rapid sequence induction. Cortisol suppression belongs to etomidate, and ketamine preserves airway reflexes.'},
{q:'Lidocaine injected around a fluctuant dental abscess gives a poor block. Why?',o:['Pus contains pseudocholinesterase that hydrolyses lidocaine','Lidocaine only works when adrenaline is added','Inflamed nerves have lost their Na⁺ channels','Acidic tissue keeps the drug ionised outside the nerve'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'local-anaesthetics',x:'Local anaesthetics are weak bases that cross the nerve membrane only in their uncharged form; the low pH of infected tissue protonates more of the drug, so little reaches the inside of the axon where it blocks the Na⁺ channel. Pseudocholinesterase hydrolyses esters, not amides such as lidocaine.'},
{q:'Minutes after a large bupivacaine field block, a woman reports a metallic taste and ringing in her ears, then has a seizure followed by bradycardia with wide QRS complexes. Besides airway support and a benzodiazepine, what is the specific treatment?',o:['20% lipid emulsion','Lidocaine bolus','Naloxone','Flumazenil'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'local-anaesthetics',x:'Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity is treated with IV lipid emulsion, which draws the lipid-soluble drug out of heart and brain. Lidocaine is itself a local anaesthetic and would add to the Na⁺ channel block, and naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not bupivacaine.'},
{q:'A man who injected heroin 4 hours ago takes his first sublingual buprenorphine dose. Within 30 minutes he is sweating and yawning, with dilated pupils, gooseflesh and cramps. Why?',o:['Buprenorphine is a full μ agonist and he is overdosed','The naloxone in the tablet is well absorbed under the tongue','A high-affinity partial agonist displaced the full agonist','He has developed serotonin syndrome'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'opioids',x:'Buprenorphine binds μ receptors more tightly than heroin but activates them only partly, so it pushes the full agonist off and the net opioid effect falls: precipitated withdrawal. Starting once withdrawal has begun avoids it. The naloxone in combined tablets is poorly absorbed sublingually and is there to deter injection.'},
{q:'A septic patient is intubated with an induction agent chosen because it barely changes heart rate or blood pressure. The next day he still needs high-dose vasopressors and his cortisol is low. Which agent was used, and what does it inhibit?',o:['Ketamine; NMDA receptors','Propofol; GABA-A receptors','Thiopental; 21-hydroxylase','Etomidate; 11β-hydroxylase'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'general-anaesthetics',x:'Etomidate is chosen for haemodynamic stability, but it inhibits adrenal 11β-hydroxylase, the last step of cortisol synthesis, and even one dose suppresses cortisol for hours. Ketamine is also used in shock, but it supports blood pressure through sympathetic activation and does not touch the adrenal enzymes.'}
]};
